## Preparation of enzymes
The expression vectors containing C140S–Q143A and N73S–Q143A hMnSOD cDNA were transformed into the sodA– ⁄ sodB– null mutant $E$ . coli strain QC774 [26]. The bacterial growth medium was supplemented with $\mathbf{0.6\mM}$ $\mathrm{{MnCl}}_{2}$ . Cells were gathered by centrifugation, lysed, heated to $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and then extensively dialyzed against buffer. Purification was achieved using FPLC on a Q-Sepharose anionexchange resin (Amersham Pharmacia Biotech) and by gel filtration on a Sephacryl S-300 column (GE Healthcare BioSciences, Piscataway, NJ). SDS ⁄ PAGE showed one intense band at $22\mathrm{\kDa}$ . The protein concentration was determined spectrophotometrically $(\varepsilon_{280}=40~500~\mathrm{M}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ . The enzyme concentration was set at the total manganese concentration determined by atomic absorption spectroscopy.
## Growth media
Rich medium was LB medium (Becton-Dickinson, Franklin Lakes, NJ). M63 minimal media, made according to Miller [39], was supplemented with $1~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ thiamine, as well as $0.5~\mathrm{mM}$ of each of the amino acids l-isoleucine, l-leucine and l-valine. For paraquat-containing media, filter-sterilized methyl viologen in the appropriately concentrated stock solution was added to the growth media after autoclaving and brief cooling, resulting in a 1000-fold dilution of the stock solution.
## Error-prone PCR and DNA shuffling
The error-prone PCR reaction contained $100~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ final volume): $10~\mathrm{mM}$ Tris ⁄ HCl $\mathrm{(pH~}8.3$ at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ), $50~\mathrm{mM}$ KCl, 7 mm $\mathbf{MgCl}_{2}$ , $0.01\%$ (w ⁄ v) gelatine, $0.2~\mathrm{mM}$ dGTP, $0.2~\mathrm{mM}$ dATP, $1\ \mathrm{mM}$ dCTP, $1\ \mathrm{mM}$ dTTP, $0.10\ \mathrm{mM}\ \mathrm{MnCl_{2}}$ , $0.5~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ both primers, $10~\mathrm{{ng}}$ of template plasmid, and $_{5\mathrm{~U~}}$ of Taq DNA polymerase. Error-prone PCR was performed in an MJ Research (Watertown, MA) PTC-200 thermocycler for 15 cycles: 1 min at $94~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $1~\mathrm{min}$ at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and $1~\mathrm{min}$ at
## Catalysis
Steady-state constants for the decay of superoxide caused by mutants of hMnSOD were measured by stopped-flow spectrophotometry (Applied Photophysics SX18.MV, Leatherhead, Surrey) based on the method of McClune and Fee [42] as modified by Greenleaf et al. [36] and by pulse radiolysis as described by Cabelli et al. [24]. Potassium superoxide was dissolved in dry dimethylsulfoxide with the solution enhanced with 18-crown-6 ether. In a dual mixing experiment, this solution was diluted 10-fold with an aqueous solution of $2.0~\mathrm{mM}$ Taps and $1.0~\mathrm{mM}$ EDTA at $\mathrm{pH}\ 11$ . After a 0.5 s delay, this superoxide solution was mixed $1:1$ $(\mathrm{v/v)}$ with buffered enzyme solution. Final solutions after mixing contained $0.5~\mathrm{mM}$ EDTA, $1.0~\mathrm{mM}$ Taps, DMSO at $4.5\mathrm{\vol.\%}$ , and $100~\mathrm{{mM}}$ of one of the following buffers: Taps $\mathrm{(pH~8.0-8.5)}$ ; Ches $\mathrm{(pH~9.0-9.5)}$ ; and Taps $\mathrm{(pH~l0.0-}$ 10.5). The superoxide concentration was varied from approximately $0.01\ \mathrm{mM}$ to $0.6\mathrm{\mM}$ , and the enzyme concentrations were near $20~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . The change in absorbance of superoxide was measured at $250~\mathrm{nm}$ $(\mathfrak{E}_{250}=2000\ \mathrm{M}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ [43]. Initial velocities were determined from the first $5-10\%$ of the reaction.
